*Customer Churn Production*
Imagine you're building this project for a telecom company like Jio or Airtel (the same concept applies to banks, Netflix, Amazon, etc.).
The project's main goal is:"Identify customers who are likely to leave the company and help the company keep them."
Instead of simply predicting Yes/No, your system acts like an intelligent assistant for the company.Let's understand it with one customer.
| Feature                | Value          |
| ---------------------- | -------------- |
| Name                   | Rahul          |
| Tenure                 | 2 months       |
| Monthly Bill           | ₹899           |
| Customer Support Calls | 5              |
| Contract               | Month-to-Month |
| Days Since Last Login  | 20             |
| Complaints             | 3              |
This information is stored in the company's database.

Step 2: Churn Prediction (Main AI Model)
The AI analyzes Rahul's data.
It notices that:New customer
High monthly bill
Many complaints
Low activity
Month-to-month contract

The model predicts:
Probability of leaving
↓
91%

Instead of saying:
Customer will leave it says,
Rahul has a 91% chance of leaving within the next month.

Step 3: Explain Why (Explainable AI)
Managers don't just want a prediction.
They want the reason.
Your system displays:
Rahul is likely to churn because:
✓ High monthly bill
✓ 3 complaints
✓ Not using the app regularly
✓ Short customer tenure

Now the manager understands why the prediction was made.

Step 4: Customer Segmentation
The system groups Rahul with similar customers.

Example:
Customer Segments
Premium Customers
Active Customers
Dormant Customers
High Risk Customers
New Customers

Rahul belongs to:
High Risk Customers. Now the company knows what type of customer he is.

Step 5: Customer Lifetime Value (CLV)
Suppose Rahul usually spends: ₹900 every month.
If he stays for 5 years, he could generate: ₹54,000

The AI predicts:
Customer Lifetime Value
₹54,000
This tells the company:
Rahul is worth keeping.
If another customer only spends ₹100/month, the company may prioritize Rahul first.

Step 6: Customer Health Score
Instead of looking at many numbers separately, your system creates one score.

Example:
Customer Health Score
35 / 100

Meaning:
Below 40 → Critical
40–70 → Moderate
Above 70 → Healthy

Rahul's score:
35
↓
Critical Customer

Step 7: Sentiment Analysis
Suppose Rahul wrote this review:
"The network is poor and customer support never solves my issues."
The AI analyzes the text.

Output:
Negative Sentiment
This increases the confidence that Rahul may leave.

Step 8: Complaint Severity Detection
Instead of counting complaints, classify them.
Complaint:
"My SIM has not worked for 4 days."

Output:
Critical Complaint
The company should respond immediately.

Step 9: Recommendation Engine
Now comes the most useful part.The AI doesn't stop at prediction. It suggests what to do.

Example:
Customer Risk
91%

--Suggested Actions
• Offer 20% discount
• Call customer personally
• Upgrade to premium support
• Provide faster complaint resolution
Now the manager knows exactly how to retain Rahul.

Step 10: Email Automation
Instead of manually contacting Rahul,
the system generates an email automatically.

Example:
Hi Rahul,

We noticed you've recently faced some issues.
As a valued customer, we'd like to offer you a 20% discount on your next bill and priority customer support.
This increases the chances that Rahul stays.

